        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-18T21:29:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is there any way that changing the value of DEVCONF_MAX could bite us? Eg, a vendor modules built with the old definition allocates something that is now too small?",
      "revId": "4f20ee71c4b46424a22db1c57f261eb5645bd172",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a4127ed9_f477b684",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/ipv6.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1594463
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-21T21:04:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am not sure it could? The vendor module will still use the old value of DEVCONF_MAX and won\u0027t know about DEVCONF_ACCEPT_RA_MIN_LFT. Any ideas, @maze@google.com?",
      "parentUuid": "571e8bdd_15db968c",
      "revId": "4f20ee71c4b46424a22db1c57f261eb5645bd172",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2076dcbb_8d3c2492",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/ipv6.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-22T02:12:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Well, so this change is definitely visible to userspace, because any time the IPv6 attributes of an interface are passed to userspace via inet6_fill_ifla6_attrs, the resulting array will be DEVCONF_MAX ingegers long.\n\nThe last of these will always be zero since __nla_reserve zeros out the memory it reserves (that\u0027s good because otherwise it might leak uninitialized kernel memory).\n\nI suppose what Todd is saying is that if a module is allocating an array that is DEVCONF_MAX integers long, and passing that to core code, the core code could \"fall off the end\" of the array and read uninitialized memory because the module only allocated something that was 4 bytes too small? Not sure if this is a practical concern though.\n\nDo we need to define DEVCONF_ACCEPT_RA_MIN_LFT in this patch? If not, it might be safer just to remove it.",
      "parentUuid": "a4127ed9_f477b684",
      "revId": "4f20ee71c4b46424a22db1c57f261eb5645bd172",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "381e0a55_c3884d35",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/ipv6.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1594463
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-22T03:35:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, but the core code never actually reads from the array, right? The only way this is used is when rtnl_fill_ifinfo calls fill_link_af. nla_reserve allocates the buffer in the skb that is later filled in ipv6_store_devconf. Am I missing something?",
      "parentUuid": "2076dcbb_8d3c2492",
      "revId": "4f20ee71c4b46424a22db1c57f261eb5645bd172",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ea49291f_7f82a527",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/ipv6.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003247
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-22T06:14:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This constant - like most (all?) _MAX constants like this at the end of an enum - is not considered immutable user API, and are regularly bumped as needed with kernel updates.\n\n(The other constants are immutable)\n\nRather this is the size of the array one needs to allocate to be able to handle all the other constants that are defined by the set of uapi headers you are using.  However you still need to validate input as being \u003c MAX - since a newer kernel may have more, and you simply cannot guaranteed that the uapi headers you built at precisely match the kernel.\n\nThere is more of an issue with kernel code building against the headers.  Here the answer is that modules should either never use _MAX constants, or be *very* careful with their use (like userspace).  Because again, they may be built with the wrong version of the headers.\n\nThere\u0027s nothing new here.",
      "parentUuid": "381e0a55_c3884d35",
      "revId": "4f20ee71c4b46424a22db1c57f261eb5645bd172",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
